{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"route53","skill_description":"Configures Amazon Route 53 DNS: public and private records, traffic-steering routing policies, health checks, DNS Firewall, Route 53 Profiles, VPC Resolver (also known as Route 53 Resolver) for hybrid and Outposts networks, and Global Resolver. Applicable when the customer wants to point a hostname at a target, split or fail over traffic across endpoints, monitor an endpoint, block malicious domains, centralize DNS across accounts, or resolve private DNS across a hybrid network. Routes to the right per-task procedure in references. Does not cover CloudFront-specific setup or non-DNS networking.\n\n\nServices: route53, vpc, outposts, cloudwatch, sns\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, network-admin, security-engineer, sre\nWorkload: networking, security, hybrid, disaster-recovery\n*","skill_name":"route53"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Network Firewall now supports Route 53 Resolved IP Validation - AWS","context":"AWS Network Firewall has introduced a new capability, Route 53 Resolved IP Validation, which extends domain-based filtering to protocols other than HTTP/HTTPS, such as SFTP and SSH. This feature ensures that a connection's destination IP address is resolved through the VPC's Amazon Route 53 VPC Resolver, preventing clients from bypassing domain rules. With this update, Network Firewall customers can implement domain filtering rules for various protocols, simplifying protection against Server Name Identifier (SNI) spoofing. The validation process verifies the IPs resolved by the Route 53 VPC Resolver against the SNI, ensuring traffic only reaches legitimately resolved destinations. Organizations can now enforce consistent domain-based security policies across all outbound traffic types. Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall can also be used in conjunction with the VPC Resolver for extended DNS security. This feature is available in all AWS commercial regions where AWS Network Firewall and Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall are available, at no additional charge. For a list of supported regions, visit the AWS Capabilities by Region page. For more information, visit the AWS Network Firewall product page and service documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/network-firewall-route-53-ip-validation/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-network-monitoring","skill_description":"Installs, configures, and troubleshoots Network Flow Monitor agents on EC2 instances to monitor network path health. Covers agent installation, IAM permissions, monitoring network paths, and troubleshooting agents reporting no metrics, HTTP 403 errors, or connectivity failures.\n\nServices: network-flow-monitor, ec2, ssm, cloudwatch\nTasks: deploy, debug\nPersona: developer, devops, network-engineer\nWorkload: networking\n*","skill_name":"aws-network-monitoring"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon ECS Service Connect enhances observability with Envoy Access Logs - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS) Service Connect has introduced support for Envoy access logs, enhancing observability into request-level traffic patterns and service interactions. This new feature captures detailed per-request telemetry for end-to-end tracing, debugging, and compliance monitoring. ECS Service Connect simplifies building secure, resilient service-to-service communication across clusters, VPCs, and AWS accounts by automatically injecting AWS-managed Envoy proxies as sidecars. These proxies handle traffic routing, load balancing, and inter-service connectivity. Envoy Access logs capture detailed traffic metadata, enabling request-level visibility into service communication patterns. This feature supports all existing application protocols (HTTP, HTTP2, GRPC, and TCP) and outputs logs to the standard output (STDOUT) stream alongside application logs. Access logs can be configured within ECS Service Connect by updating the ServiceConnectConfiguration to enable access logging. Query strings are redacted by default to protect sensitive data. This feature is available in all regions where Amazon ECS Service Connect is supported. For more information, visit the Amazon ECS Developer Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/amazon-ecs-service-connect-envoy-access-logs/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-networking","skill_description":"Routes AWS networking requests to the correct service skill for implementation. Covers Route 53 (DNS, health checks, routing policies, Resolver, DNS Firewall), CloudFront (caching, edge, OAC, mTLS, signed URLs), Transit Gateway (multi-VPC hub, segmentation, centralized egress), Direct Connect (hybrid link, DX Gateway, MACsec), Site-to-Site VPN (IPsec tunnels, static or BGP), WAF (web ACLs, AWS Managed Rules, rate-based rules, Bot and Fraud Control), and Shield Advanced (L3/L4 DDoS). Applicable when creating, configuring, troubleshooting, or designing across these services, choosing between them, or diagnosing connectivity or traffic-filtering issues. Not for VPC subnets and route tables, load balancers, VPC endpoints, PrivateLink, API Gateway, IAM policy logic, container or serverless networking, or IaC authoring.\n\nServices: route53, route53resolver, cloudfront, directconnect, transitgateway, vpn, waf, shield\nTasks: configure, troubleshoot, debug, deploy, secure, migrate\nPersona: developer, network-engineer, devops, security-engineer\nWorkload: networking, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-networking"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether VPC Flow Logs capture DNS traffic to the VPC resolver"}
